WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.440
After my recent video about Rhystic Study, I&nbsp;
got a lot of comments saying one specific thing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.160
what about Sol Ring? Why is it that Rhystic Study&nbsp;
should be banned, but not this all-powerful fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.800
mana piece? Bits and pieces of my answer to that&nbsp;
question are sitting around in plain sight—as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.040
mention in that video, the reason to ban&nbsp;
things is having a high power level plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.440
having a bothersome play pattern, and I find&nbsp;
the play pattern of Sol Ring less bothersome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.120
than that of Rhystic. Not a very high bar, but&nbsp;
the ring has cleared it. I also had a segment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.440
a 30 minute defense of tutors I put out last&nbsp;
year where I discussed what causes cards to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.560
feel problematic, and why a very early game&nbsp;
threat like Sol Ring feels less game-warping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.800
than other very powerful cards that exist. Not&nbsp;
un-game-warping, but less game-warping. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.160
I think it’s worth returning to the question&nbsp;
of Sol Ring and asking some questions about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.800
and giving the question of whether it should&nbsp;
stay legal a fair shake. I still firmly lean on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.560
the side of not banning it currently, and I’ll&nbsp;
be laying out my reasons for feeling that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.040
but the arguments in favor of a ban are also&nbsp;
fascinating, and I love being fascinated.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.560
Sol Ring is a card that can really jump-start an&nbsp;
aggressive gameplan, and if you’re looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.560
other ways to get your speed on, look no further&nbsp;
than the Red Collection from this video’s sponsor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.000
Voidmonster. Voidmonster.org is a TCG-inspired&nbsp;
accessory and apparel shop started by artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.960
Forrest Imel, and every month there’s a new drop&nbsp;
featuring some fantastic original designs that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.240
only available to preorder for a week. This week&nbsp;
is the Red Collection, which features a deck box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.480
sleeves, a playmat, and a card binder,&nbsp;
all with variations of this red pattern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.480
with these sort of rock-y, magma-y&nbsp;
details that I really dig. As always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.360
these are some super cool accessories&nbsp;
available for only a limited time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.640
and if you’re interested in them you should&nbsp;
follow my link in the description and preorder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.440
by March 1st and noon PST. Thanks to&nbsp;
Voidmonster for sponsoring the video!

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.120
Okay, so we’re going to start with the play&nbsp;
patterns of Sol Ring, and why I find them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.920
sometimes problematic but unobjectionable a lot&nbsp;
of the rest of the time. The card’s most obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.720
feature is that it warps early games. Turn 1&nbsp;
Sol Ring, as well as its mean older brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.480
turn 1 Sol Ring Arcane Signet, can really set up&nbsp;
a player for a nasty turn 2 and turn 3. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.400
most casual EDH players have been flattened&nbsp;
by a killer Sol Ring rollout in their time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.200
but that early-ness of Sol Ring is actually a&nbsp;
reason why I don’t always mind it so much. Turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.640
1 Sol Ring is amazing, and turn 2 Sol Ring is also&nbsp;
pretty juicy, but what if you draw into it on turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.080
3? How about turn 4 or 5? At a certain point you&nbsp;
might not even want to draw into this little guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.440
and this points to something important about the&nbsp;
card: its ephemeral nature. To briefly return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.200
a metaphor I used last year, Sol Ring is&nbsp;
strongest when it comes earliest in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.760
pipeline toward victory, but it falls off fast&nbsp;
compared to a lot of other especially powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.400
cards. Smothering Tithe is another card that&nbsp;
offers a lot of mana for its cost, but in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.400
case the mana is dynamic in how quickly it&nbsp;
gets generated, it’s storable, and since it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.360
in the form of treasures it can also enable some&nbsp;
powerful artifact synergies. It’s still just mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.800
but it’s a decidedly more scalable form of mana.&nbsp;
Next up along the pipeline might be a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.360
draw engine like Rhystic Study or The One Ring—a&nbsp;
strong flow of cards will almost always be useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.520
whether that be in an early setup turn, or for&nbsp;
when a player is drawing their way toward a kill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.840
The potency of one of these could be limited by&nbsp;
the amount of mana a player has, but drawing cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.000
can also draw into ways to solve a player’s&nbsp;
mana issues, so for most decks this will be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.960
temporary limitation, if even one at all. Finally,&nbsp;
we could talk about a powerful endgame card that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.720
will usually spell out victory if a player is&nbsp;
ahead, and could give a shot at swinging the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.840
back around if they’re behind. Bolas’s Citadel is&nbsp;
a very powerful card, and the point in a player’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.800
gameplan where it fits in is often quite far&nbsp;
along, just before a player is looking to win.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.240
Alright, so what am I trying to say&nbsp;
by constructing this scale? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.720
it’s a useful data point for analyzing cards that&nbsp;
most people would consider very powerful. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.960
consider how often a Bolas’s Citadel will feel&nbsp;
especially powerful, maybe even too powerful for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.600
some games. The answer to that might vary by&nbsp;
table, but I’m willing to bet it’s reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.560
frequent that this card feels a bit out of place.&nbsp;
That’s not any sort of complaint about the card—I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.280
love Bolas’s Citadel—but it is an interesting&nbsp;
fact about it, and it’s a fact that has a lot to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.240
do with the point in the game at which this card&nbsp;
is powerful. If a Bolas’s Citadel hits the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.240
the game will usually be over if its controller&nbsp;
is allowed to untap with it, and it might even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.400
be over the same turn it was played with the&nbsp;
right setup. This means that the social tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.640
of the EDH format—the fact that you have 2&nbsp;
other opponents in the game, basically—aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.520
very useful for dealing with Bolas’s Citadel.&nbsp;
If people happen to have big boards right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.280
and are able to get an opportunity to swing&nbsp;
big at the Bolas’s Citadel player, that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.920
be a way of containing the card, but it’s kinda&nbsp;
rare that your options will be this good unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.560
the Citadel-haver played it when they were pretty&nbsp;
far behind. More realistically, the other players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.800
at the table will get maybe a single turn cycle to&nbsp;
either come up with a piece of artifact removal or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.360
have their own crazy pop-off turn, and if neither&nbsp;
of those things happen, you’re kind of cooked.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.280
This is in contrast to the way things play out&nbsp;
with a card that is powerful earlier in the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.600
A powerful draw engine like Braids or Toski can&nbsp;
generate a lot of card advantage if not addressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.960
but these are significantly more manageable&nbsp;
through the social nature of the format since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.800
they typically won’t be winning the game super&nbsp;
imminently. If somebody is getting a lot of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.320
off of one of these, their opponents can attack&nbsp;
them and/or interact with some of their stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.960
in order to slow them down. This is even more true&nbsp;
for a mana tool like Sol Ring that comes into play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.000
early—it will absolutely accelerate the gameplan&nbsp;
of its caster, but in a bracket 2 or 3 game there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.560
will still be a lot of turns for the table to work&nbsp;
together against the Sol Ringing player before a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.120
win is being threatened. To be clear, this is&nbsp;
not to say Sol Ring will never be a problem. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.200
the player who draws it is playing the strongest&nbsp;
deck, if that player draws other powerful stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.520
in conjunction with the ring, if one or more&nbsp;
of their opponents has a rough start—all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.800
these things could result in Sol Ring causing a&nbsp;
blowout, turning an uphill battle into a non-game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:39.200
This does happen sometimes, but it’s not the norm&nbsp;
of the card. More frequently, players do get ample&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.840
opportunity to gang up on the Sol Ring player,&nbsp;
and that’s assuming it’s a problem at all. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.160
a player has kept a questionable hand with a Sol&nbsp;
Ring in it, and in those cases it’s pretty common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.920
to see a scary first three turns followed&nbsp;
by a rather unconcerning turns 4, 5, and 6.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.080
Another factor that it’s worth considering with&nbsp;
regard to banning a card is how much a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.480
dominates the landscape of a particular game&nbsp;
it’s in. If the legends about Primeval Titan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.640
are to be believed, it basically transformed the&nbsp;
commander format into the Primeval Titan format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.320
for a year or so, with everybody playing&nbsp;
it and stealing it and copying it in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.720
to get all their favorite lands out onto the&nbsp;
board. The same goes for Prophet of Kruphix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:19.200
instantly transforming a game into a Prophet&nbsp;
of Kruphix game the second it hit the field.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.200
This can even be the case for Rhystic Study—a&nbsp;
player getting to draw a lot of extra cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.200
might trivialize any other set of value engines&nbsp;
they were playing, turning their gameplan from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.240
*insert complex synergy web here* into Rhystic&nbsp;
Study, end of story. In contrast to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.760
examples, Sol Ring is humble and task-oriented. It&nbsp;
enables a player’s gameplan early on, but the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.800
lacks any sort of identity or scaling, so after&nbsp;
a few turns it often slips into the background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.360
I’ve had multiple games recently where I entirely&nbsp;
forgot it had been played, only remembering about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.080
it after a moment of marveling at how sleek and&nbsp;
tuned an opponent’s deck had seemed. To be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.240
this isn’t necessarily a perfect defense of a&nbsp;
powerful card—you could make similar arguments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.880
about Mana Crypt and Jeweled Lotus, and I’m&nbsp;
perfectly happy with them staying banned. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.200
I do see some appeal in the fact that this is a&nbsp;
universally available tool that can give players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.920
a chance to play an accelerated version of their&nbsp;
gameplan. To me, getting to be the big-bad is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.560
of the defining experiences of playing EDH, and&nbsp;
it’s an experience that everybody deserves access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.600
to. Now, just to be clear, I have seen some bad&nbsp;
defenses of Sol Ring that argue that it’s a tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.440
of the people, an equalizing factor between the&nbsp;
stronger players and the weaker players out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.560
or the stronger decks and the weaker decks. These&nbsp;
are bad defenses because anybody can draw Sol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.200
Ring, including the strongest player, and I prefer&nbsp;
a modified defense of the card that focuses on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.200
ability to increase early game variance. Increased&nbsp;
variance won’t necessarily create fairer games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.600
nor is “fairness” a particularly good metric for&nbsp;
analyzing commander games in the first place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.880
but what it can do is create more balanced&nbsp;
play experiences on the aggregate, offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.080
exciting games to players playing decks that might&nbsp;
otherwise be slower. And, that’s worth something.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.760
So ultimately, I’ve grown a bit more tolerant&nbsp;
of Sol Ring over the past couple years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:03.280
even as I remain a fairly variance-averse player.&nbsp;
Random power spikes in a gameplan can make for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.080
frustratingly unpredictable play experiences,&nbsp;
making it impossible to predict opposing decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.960
and strategize. A deck looking very different&nbsp;
power-wise game-to-game can also make it tricky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.280
to set the table up for success, to find a pairing&nbsp;
of decks that will result in something satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.480
for everyone involved. In principle this logic&nbsp;
could provide a reason for me to hate Sol Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.880
but the profile of the card manages to minimize&nbsp;
some of the issues that significant power level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.080
variance can cause. Being strong early means it is&nbsp;
a thing that can be considered strategically right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.320
from the outset of a game, and this feature also&nbsp;
offers plenty of time for players to collaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.960
in dealing with a Sol-Ring-fueled gameplan.&nbsp;
Being a background enabler whose presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.920
a game diminishes over time does mitigate some&nbsp;
of the power spike it provides—a deck that drew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.080
Sol Ring will usually feel much faster, but&nbsp;
it will still feel like a version of itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.040
rather than a midrange deck that has transformed&nbsp;
into a combo deck or something. The one remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.800
issue I see with Sol Ring is that sometimes&nbsp;
the strongest deck at the table will get it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.200
and that deck will then get to wipe the&nbsp;
floor in a way it might not have otherwise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.760
This undeniably does add to the difficulty&nbsp;
in constructing successful commander games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.160
adds to the uncertainty of how much power level&nbsp;
difference between decks is too much. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.320
that fact overshadows the other aspects&nbsp;
of the card I brought up is up to you.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.720
Alright, let’s move on to the logistics of&nbsp;
banning Sol Ring. I wanted to separate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:25.040
theoretical case for banning Sol Ring from the&nbsp;
practical case for banning it, in large part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.680
because I find the practical case for banning&nbsp;
Sol Ring almost impossible to really consider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:35.600
an insurmountably challenging proposition. It’s&nbsp;
been printed in every single precon since 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:41.120
and it’s played in over 80% of decks according&nbsp;
to EDHREC. Banning Sol Ring would be a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.520
and monumental undertaking, requiring a&nbsp;
prolonged communication campaign to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.000
all sectors of the playerbase. I think we as&nbsp;
EDH players on the internet have a tendency to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.560
assume the community as a whole is far more&nbsp;
cohesive and engaged than they really are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.240
likely surely these spicy musings on Reddit&nbsp;
or YouTube are an accurate reflection of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.120
playerbase. But no, the EDH format is full of&nbsp;
people who don’t consume much content about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.640
who haven’t heard the latest announcement about&nbsp;
the bracket system, who don’t pay much attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.800
to current sets, who don’t spend a whole lot of&nbsp;
time building decks, and who don’t get together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.840
to play more than once a month, if that. Take a&nbsp;
moment to think about it—for a 60 card format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.680
the people playing it competitively will be&nbsp;
actively listening for ban updates, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.560
that affects the metagame and also happens a lot&nbsp;
now. We can also think about the average commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.680
player who ran Jeweled Lotus or Mana Crypt or even&nbsp;
the still-legal Rhystic Study—these players will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.320
be relatively in-tune with the commander format&nbsp;
compared to most, and will be able to actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.680
receive any news about these cards getting banned.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, if Sol Ring was banned, the median Sol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:46.640
Ring player would probably hear about it 3&nbsp;
months later, and go “oh, why? Okay. Guess I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.360
have some swaps to make.” In addition to being&nbsp;
challenging to fully convey to the playerbase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.920
banning Sol Ring would also fundamentally muddy&nbsp;
all Wizards of the Coast communication about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:00.880
commander format. Precons serve as an example of&nbsp;
what Commander deckbuilding looks like, the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:06.720
it should use, the features it should have, and on&nbsp;
a fundamental level, how the format works. To ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.680
a card that WotC has chosen to put in every precon&nbsp;
would destroy any sort of messaging consistency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.560
about what the format means, and would make any&nbsp;
future messaging power significantly weaker.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.200
In this way, I do think part of the discussion&nbsp;
of a Sol Ring ban is a sign of the times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.920
A lot of commander players kinda want to undermine&nbsp;
WotC’s relationship with the commander format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.360
which feels weird to say out loud but I don’t&nbsp;
think the people who feel that way would disagree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.320
with that assessment. It is also, admittedly,&nbsp;
very funny to demand that Hasbro does something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:39.520
as supremely stupid as banning Sol Ring, and after&nbsp;
all the destructive profit-driven decisions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.040
past few years it’s hard to deny that there&nbsp;
might be a little shadenfreude in watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.760
them try and recover from that fumble. But the&nbsp;
angst that bubbles in the hearts of us terminally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.040
online commander players isn’t great as a genuine&nbsp;
justification of something like a Sol Ring ban. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.280
the real world, weighing a ban unfortunately&nbsp;
does need to involve considering the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:02.160
of that ban in practical terms, in addition to&nbsp;
theoretical reasoning. In the case of Sol Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.640
on a theoretical level I’m relatively ambivalent&nbsp;
about it. I see the issues that the card has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.440
and its very high power level, but I also see&nbsp;
some genuine reasons why people like it, so my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:16.480
feelings probably end up basically a wash. When we&nbsp;
consider the practical consequences, though, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:21.120
kinda just an obvious nonstarter. A card that has&nbsp;
been printed and handed to the casual playerbase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.760
as much as Sol Ring would have to be noxious&nbsp;
to be worth going to the trouble of banning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:31.120
utterly miserable and pleasure-destroying on every&nbsp;
possible level. And, whatever issues you may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.080
with Sol Ring, they probably don’t rise to that&nbsp;
level. This does bring up a question, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:41.600
what exactly constitutes a good practical reason&nbsp;
not to ban a card? Being extremely widely printed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.760
to players who are on the most casual fringes of a&nbsp;
format seems like a pretty good logistical reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:52.080
not to ban a card to me, but what about other&nbsp;
things? How far do practicality arguments extend?

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.680
One of these practical reasons to keep&nbsp;
a card unbanned might be popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.480
and my reasoning around Sol Ring is arguably a&nbsp;
version of this. However, realistically what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.800
care about most heavily here is inclusion in&nbsp;
precons. If a card has shown up in more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.880
half of precon sets ever released I’m going&nbsp;
to be a little leery of banning it since it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.120
been purposefully put into the hands of a lot of&nbsp;
more casual players, but if it’s just a card that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.600
a lot of people happen to play? That could be&nbsp;
perfectly fine. Cards do need to be some degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.040
of popular in the first place to be ban-worthy,&nbsp;
after all, we’re not going to ban something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:26.000
has no presence and therefore no impact. Another&nbsp;
practical consideration that sometimes comes up is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.440
price. A card being expensive is a reason not to&nbsp;
ban it, so the reasoning could go, because banning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.640
it will financially impact players and game-stores&nbsp;
alike, destroying value for no good reason. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.760
problem with this reason is extremely simple:&nbsp;
it also applies to reprints. Every price-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:46.160
argument for not banning something like Mana&nbsp;
Crypt, or for not banning Rhystic Study now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:51.040
also works as an argument not to reprint these&nbsp;
cards for fear of tanking the price that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.360
and that’s kind of a problem, right? Most people&nbsp;
would consider it a good thing for Wizards of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.920
the Coast to print more of the cards people want&nbsp;
to play with. You could consider a ban somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.920
optically different than mass reprints, like&nbsp;
it’s seen as a somewhat more aggressive maneuver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:08.560
by the playerbase, but that’s splitting hairs&nbsp;
as far as I’m concerned. The same price-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.960
logic is getting employed and it’s not any sort&nbsp;
of logic that deserves to see the light of day.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:18.080
A final practical reason worth discussing is&nbsp;
iconic-ness, how symbolically relevant a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:22.480
is on the scale of the commander format. In my&nbsp;
Rhystic Study video I laid out why I don’t put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:26.720
a whole lot of salt into the iconic-ness of&nbsp;
a card, but at the same time I suspected that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.360
Rhystic Study would stick around for at least a&nbsp;
bit longer on the back of its iconic status. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:35.840
the ban announcement 4 days later confirmed that&nbsp;
prediction. While the card isn’t quite as iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:40.160
as Sol Ring, there is an undeniable pedigree&nbsp;
there. It’s a card that was printed 6 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:44.240
before the formation of the Rules Committee, a&nbsp;
card whose strange design made it dead on arrival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.640
in 1v1 formats but also offered it a chance&nbsp;
at new life in the 4 player commander format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:52.400
It’s a card that’s powerful, that’s widely&nbsp;
played, and that draws attention to itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:56.960
by its very design. None of these are&nbsp;
bulletproof reasons not to ban a card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:00.800
and indeed I find them inadequate as&nbsp;
reasons to keep Rhystic Study legal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:05.280
but it’s also not hard for me to imagine why other&nbsp;
people might find them adequate. The question of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:09.760
whether to ban Rhystic Study, just as it does&nbsp;
with Sol Ring or any other card, comes down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:13.920
how problematic it is on a play-pattern level&nbsp;
versus how challenging and harmful a ban would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:18.640
be on a practical level. Ultimately, I wouldn’t&nbsp;
be surprised to see a Rhystic Study ban a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:23.840
ways down the line, but that’ll require the&nbsp;
theoretical reasons to ban it surpassing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.240
practical reasons not to ban it in the minds of&nbsp;
the community in general and the Commander Format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.880
Panel in specific. I certainly have strong&nbsp;
feelings about those theoretical reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.440
but my feelings are not the consensus opinion—yet.&nbsp;
We can call that a work in progress, I suppose.

